<!-- ================================================================ GENETICS -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-genetics" data-name="genetics">
{{ plain('genetics') }}
<h2>Does genetically higher protein or expression change UC risk?</h2>
<p class="lede">Mendelian randomisation uses inherited DNA variants that change how much of a gene product a person makes as a natural experiment. It is run three ways, with the same outcome (the UC GWAS of de Lange et al. 2017) and the same rules (fine-mapped cis credible sets from Open Targets 26.09, lead variant p &lt; 5×10⁻⁸, MHC and palindromic variants removed, Wald ratio or inverse-variance weighted estimate): with protein in <strong>blood</strong> (UK Biobank Pharma Proteomics Project), with gene expression in the <strong>colon wall</strong> (GTEx v10 transverse and sigmoid colon, CEDAR transverse colon and rectum biopsies) and with expression in <strong>single gut cell types</strong> (IBDverse, biopsies of people with and without IBD). Significant genes are checked for a shared genetic signal with an IBD GWAS (colocalisation).</p>
<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ "{:,}".format(G.plasma.tested) }}</b><span>plasma proteins tested; {{ G.plasma.significant }} significant, {{ G.plasma.colocalising }} colocalise</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ "{:,}".format(G.colon.tested) }}</b><span>genes tested with colon eQTL; {{ G.colon.significant }} significant, {{ G.colon.colocalising }} colocalise</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ "{:,}".format(G.cell.tested) }}</b><span>genes tested in gut cell types; {{ G.cell.significant }} significant, {{ G.cell.colocalising }} colocalise</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ G.plasma.clinical_significant | length }} / {{ G.plasma.clinical_tested }}</b><span>UC clinical targets tested in plasma that are significant</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ G.plasma.finngen_replicated }} / {{ G.plasma.significant }}</b><span>significant plasma results that replicate in FinnGen</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ G.colon.finngen_replicated + G.cell.finngen_replicated }} / {{ G.colon.significant + G.cell.significant }}</b><span>significant gut results that replicate in FinnGen</span></div>
</div>
{% if third.liu %}<p class="small">Largest GWAS: with Liu et al. 2023 (23,252 UC cases, European and East Asian; it includes the samples of de Lange 2017 and FinnGen, so it is reported apart and not combined), {{ third.liu.plasma.rep }} of {{ third.liu.plasma.n }} significant plasma proteins and {{ third.liu.gut.rep }} of {{ third.liu.gut.n }} significant gut estimates replicate.</p>{% endif %}
{% if third.mr_sens %}{% set Ms = third.mr_sens %}
<h3>Sensitivity analyses</h3>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th class="num">Significant</th><th class="num">With ≥ 3 instruments</th><th class="num">No directional pleiotropy (MR-Egger intercept p ≥ 0.05)</th><th class="num">Weighted median agrees</th><th class="num">Heterogeneous instruments (p &lt; 0.05)</th><th class="num">Steiger direction correct</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for k, label in [("plasma_protein", "Plasma protein"), ("colon_tissue", "Colon tissue"), ("gut_cell", "Gut cell types")] %}{% set v = Ms[k] %}<tr><td>{{ label }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.significant }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.with_3plus_instruments }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.egger_intercept_ok }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.weighted_median_agrees }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.heterogeneity_p05 }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.steiger_correct }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">MR-Egger and the weighted median need at least three independent instruments, which most gut estimates do not have (one fine-mapped eQTL per gene); for them only the Steiger direction (the variant explains more of the exposure than of UC) can be checked, and it holds for all. Heterogeneity is expected for proteins with several independent cis signals.</p>{% endif %}
<p class="small">Replication: every estimate is repeated with a second, independent UC GWAS (FinnGen R12, Finnish biobank, no sample overlap with de Lange 2017). A result replicates when the FinnGen estimate has the same sign and p &lt; 0.05; the two outcomes are also combined by inverse variance (combined q in the evidence records).</p>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('mr_plasma', figs.mr) }}
  {{ plot('mr_colon', figs.mr_colon) }}
  {{ plot('mr_agree', figs.mr_agree) }}
  {{ plot('mr_cells', figs.mr_cells) }}
  {{ plot('finngen', figs.finngen) }}
</div>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How to read these charts</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Volcano plots.</strong> Each dot is a gene. Right of zero: the genetically higher level raises UC risk (red when significant); left: it lowers risk (blue). Higher dots have stronger evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Blood against gut.</strong> For genes with instruments in both, the two estimates are plotted against each other. Agreement in direction supports the result; disagreement can be real biology (a gene acting differently in blood cells and in the gut wall).</li>
<li><strong>Cell types.</strong> For candidate genes with a cell-type instrument, the estimate in each gut cell type. It shows in which cell the genetic effect is seen.</li></ul></div>
<h3>Significant plasma proteins {{ q('mr_table') }}</h3>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Protein (gene)</th><th class="num">OR per SD</th><th class="num">95 % CI</th><th class="num">p</th><th class="num">q</th><th class="num">Instruments</th><th>Colocalises</th><th>FinnGen (✓ replicates)</th><th>UC clinical target</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in extra.mr %}<tr><td><b>{{ r.gene }}</b></td><td class="num">{{ r.or }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.ci[0] }}–{{ r.ci[1] }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.p }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.q }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.n }}</td><td>{{ r.coloc }}</td><td>{{ G.plasma_finngen.get(r.gene, "—") }}</td><td>{{ "yes" if r.clinical else "" }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Significant genes, colon expression {{ q('mr_table') }}</h3>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Gene</th><th>Tissue</th><th class="num">OR per SD</th><th class="num">q</th><th class="num">Instruments</th><th>Colocalises</th><th>FinnGen (✓ replicates)</th><th>Plasma estimate</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in G.colon_rows %}<tr><td><b>{{ r.gene }}</b></td><td>{{ r.label }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.or }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.q }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.n }}</td><td>{{ r.coloc }}</td><td>{{ r.finngen }}</td><td>{{ r.plasma or "—" }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
<h3>The leading candidates in all three layers</h3>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Gene</th><th>Plasma protein</th><th>Colon expression</th><th>Gut cell type (strongest)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in G.candidates %}<tr><td><b>{{ r.gene }}</b></td><td>{{ r.plasma }}</td><td>{{ r.colon }}</td><td>{{ r.cell }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">"Not tested" means the gene has no genetic instrument in that layer; it is not evidence against the gene. Colon eQTL come from people without IBD (GTEx) or healthy volunteers (CEDAR); IBDverse includes IBD patients. For the cell layer, the p-value of the strongest cell type is multiplied by the number of cell types tested for that gene before the correction over genes.</p>
{{ findings('external', 'External-knowledge agent findings') }}
{% set AG = G.alphagenome %}{% if AG %}{% set V = AG.summary.validation_atlas %}{% set V2 = AG.summary.validation_ag2 %}
<h3 id="alphagenome">Which gene and cell does a UC risk variant act on? (AlphaGenome) {{ q('alphagenome') }}</h3>
<p>Mendelian randomisation needs a measured eQTL or pQTL, which most genes lack. AlphaGenome (Google DeepMind) predicts from the DNA sequence how a variant changes RNA in a tissue or cell type. The UC GWAS of Liu et al. 2023 has {{ AG.summary.loci }} risk loci outside the MHC; each was fine-mapped approximately (one causal variant per locus, Wakefield Bayes factors), giving {{ "{:,}".format(AG.summary.credible_set_variants) }} variants in the 95 % credible sets. For each locus and gene, the predicted effect of the <strong>risk allele</strong> is the mean of its variants' predicted effects weighted by their probability of being causal: in colon tissue and blood immune cells (AlphaGenome Atlas) and in twelve colon cell types (AlphaGenome 2).</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Check against measured, fine-mapped eQTL</th><th class="num">Pairs</th><th class="num">Same direction, all</th><th class="num">Same direction, strongest quarter</th><th class="num">Spearman</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Atlas, colon tissue vs GTEx colon eQTL</td><td class="num">{{ V.pairs }}</td><td class="num">{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * V.agreement_all) }} %</td><td class="num">{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * V.agreement_top_25pct) }} %</td><td class="num">{{ V.spearman }}</td></tr>
<tr><td>AlphaGenome 2, matched colon cell type vs IBDverse cell-type eQTL</td><td class="num">{{ V2.sc_expression_matched.pairs }}</td><td class="num">{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * V2.sc_expression_matched.agreement_all) }} %</td><td class="num">{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * V2.sc_expression_matched.agreement_top_quarter) }} %</td><td class="num">{{ V2.sc_expression_matched.spearman }}</td></tr>
<tr><td>AlphaGenome 2, wrong cell type (same eQTL)</td><td class="num">{{ V2.sc_expression_mismatched.pairs }}</td><td class="num">{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * V2.sc_expression_mismatched.agreement_all) }} %</td><td class="num">{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * V2.sc_expression_mismatched.agreement_top_quarter) }} %</td><td class="num">{{ V2.sc_expression_mismatched.spearman }}</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">Strong predictions have the right direction and weak ones are close to chance. Direction is not enough to say <strong>which</strong> gene a variant acts on: near a measured eQTL variant, 2.4 % of the other genes also pass the cell-type size threshold, about five genes per variant over 15 cell types.</p>
<p><strong>Result.</strong> A first version of this section listed genes by the size of their predicted change alone and named CCL20 and IL10. That reading is withdrawn: with a rule that also identifies the gene (the largest predicted change at its variant, at least twice the next gene), CCL20 and IL10 are not assigned. The corrected analysis, over {{ GT.summary.signals if GT else "all" }} risk signals and all cell types, is the <strong>Genetics first</strong> list (Findings, New targets): {{ GT.summary.genes if GT else "few" }} genes.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Limits and licence.</strong> These are predictions from DNA sequence, not measurements, and the fine-mapping ignores linkage between variants. AlphaGenome outputs are for non-commercial research use only and not for clinical decisions (AlphaGenome Terms of Service); unlike the rest of this report, they are not offered under CC BY 4.0. Cite Avsec et al. 2026 (Nature) and the AlphaGenome Atlas preprint.</div>
{% endif %}
</section>

