</div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
{% if D.repurposing %}<h3>Repurposing leads: drugs for other diseases, never tried in UC</h3>
<p class="small">The first-in-class filter sets aside every gene that already has a clinical drug. Of those, {{ D.repurposing | length }} have drugs only for other diseases, with no trial in UC, Crohn's disease or IBD: the target can be reached and its safety in people is partly known, so they are candidates to repurpose. They pass the same tissue-change filter as the first-in-class genes. These genes rise in UC, so what matters is whether an <strong>inhibitor</strong> exists: {{ D.repurposing | selectattr("inhibitor") | list | length }} have one in the clinic. For the others the drugs activate the protein, or their action is not stated, which is often an antibody that carries a toxin to cells bearing the protein (cancer drugs that would harm the colon lining). A drug made for cancer may also be too toxic for a chronic disease. Genes with an inhibitor come first, then by UC papers (least studied first); drugs that hit more than five targets are not counted. Those with an inhibitor are scored on the Repurposing page.</p>
