{{ plain('validation') }}
<h2>Independent validation and robustness</h2>
<p class="lede">Expression differences between UC and control colon are only one kind of evidence, so this tab collects the others: replication in independent adult and paediatric cohorts, the composition adjustment that separates tissue change from cell influx, causal genetics in blood and gut, and transcription-factor activity.</p>
{{ paras(f.narrative.sections.validation or []) }}
<div class="howbox"><h4>The four charts below, in plain words</h4><ol>
<li><strong>External replication.</strong> Each dot is a gene. Horizontal: its change in MSCCR. Vertical: its change in UNIFI, a different set of adult patients measured by microarray. Dots along the diagonal agree.</li>
<li><strong>Cell influx or tissue change.</strong> For each gene, one marker shows the change in the whole biopsy and another the change after adjusting for the mix of cells. If the second stays far from zero, the tissue itself changes. If it collapses, arriving cells were carrying it.</li>
<li><strong>Blood and gut genetics.</strong> Each dot is a gene tested with instruments in both: the causal estimate from plasma protein and from colon expression.</li>
<li><strong>Transcription factors.</strong> Each bar is a regulator. Longer bars mean its known target genes move together in inflamed mucosa, so it is probably more active.</li></ol></div>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('replication_scatter', figs.cohort_agree) }}
  {{ plot('composition_shift', figs.composition_shift) }}
  {{ plot('genetics_agree', figs.mr_agree) }}
  {{ plot('tf_bars', figs.tf_bars) }}
</div>

<h2>Does the signal replicate in held-out cohorts?</h2>
<p class="lede">MSCCR drives most of the meta-analysis. The two paediatric RNA-seq cohorts, PROTECT and RISK, are separate patients (treatment-naive children), sequenced in other centres. The discovery changes of MSCCR replicate in the same direction at q &lt; 0.05 in {% for r in replication if r.cohort in ("GSE109142", "GSE117993") %}{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * r.share) }} % ({{ cohort_names[r.cohort] }}, rho {{ "%.2f" | format(r.rho) }}){% if not loop.last %} and {% endif %}{% endfor %} of tested genes.</p>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('confirmatory', figs.confirm_protect) }}
  {{ plot('confirmatory', figs.confirm_risk) }}
</div>
<h3>Candidates in the held-out cohorts</h3>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Gene</th><th>MSCCR log2FC</th><th>PROTECT log2FC (q)</th><th>RISK log2FC (q)</th><th>Replicates</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in confirm_rows %}<tr><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td class="num">{{ r.msccr }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.protect }} ({{ r.protect_q }})</td><td class="num">{{ r.risk }} ({{ r.risk_q }})</td><td>{{ r.replicates }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>

{% if third.proteomics %}{% set P = third.proteomics %}
<h2>Does the change hold at protein level? {{ q('proteomics') }}</h2>
<p class="lede">Everything so far is RNA. An independent proteomics study ({{ P.study }}) measured proteins in colon mucosa biopsies of untreated UC patients at diagnosis and healthy controls (label-free mass spectrometry; other patients than any RNA cohort). Of the {{ P.rna_changes_tested }} genes with a clear RNA change (meta-analysis padj &lt; 0.05, |log2FC| ≥ 1) whose protein was quantified, {{ P.replicated }} ({{ "%.0f" | format(100 * P.replication_rate) }} %) change again at protein level in the same direction (padj &lt; 0.05), {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * P.replication_rate_log2fc_ge2) }} % when the RNA change is at least four-fold. Over {{ "{:,}".format(P.genes_compared) }} genes the RNA and protein changes correlate at rho {{ P.spearman_log2fc }}.</p>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('proteomics', figs.proteomics) }}
  <div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Gene</th><th class="num">RNA log2FC</th><th class="num">Protein log2 ratio</th><th class="num">Protein padj</th><th>Replicates</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {% for r in third.proteomics_rows %}<tr{% if r.evidence_ids %} class="claim" data-evidence="{{ r.evidence_ids | evattr }}"{% endif %}><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td class="num">{{ r.rna | fmt }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.protein | fmt }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.padj }}</td><td>{{ r.replicates }}</td><td>{{ r.evidence_ids | cite | safe }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table></div>
</div>
<p class="small">Many candidates (CXCR2, small secreted proteins) are not quantified by mass spectrometry of whole biopsies; a missing protein is a gap in the data, not a failed replication.</p>{% endif %}

{% if third.mouse %}{% set M = third.mouse %}
<h2>Do mouse models agree? {{ q('mouse') }}</h2>
<p class="lede">Mouse knockouts are an independent, experimental line of evidence. In MGI, {{ M.genes_with_colitis_phenotype }} genes have a single-gene mouse model (conditional knockouts included) with spontaneous colitis, worse or milder induced colitis. That is {{ "%.1f" | format(100 * M.background_rate) }} % of genes with a mouse ortholog, against {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * M.clinical_targets_rate) }} % of UC clinical targets, {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * M.convergence_top100_rate) }} % of the convergence top 100 and {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * M.target_profile_top100_rate) }} % of the target-profile top 100: the rankings are about ten times enriched for genes with a colitis phenotype, although no mouse data entered them. Most leading candidates ({{ third.mouse_candidates_without[:12] | join(", ") }}) have no recorded colitis model: untested, not negative.</p>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('mouse', figs.mouse) }}
  <div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Gene</th><th>Direction</th><th class="num">Spontaneous</th><th class="num">Worse induced</th><th class="num">Protected</th><th class="num">Mouse colitis papers</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  {% for r in third.mouse_rows %}<tr{% if r.evidence_ids %} class="claim" data-evidence="{{ r.evidence_ids | evattr }}"{% endif %}><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td>{{ r.direction }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.spont }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.worse }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.protected }}</td><td class="num">{{ (third.mouse_lit or {}).get(r.gene, "—") }}</td><td>{{ r.evidence_ids | cite | safe }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
  </tbody></table></div>
</div>
{% if third.mouse_lit_rows %}<h3>Tested in mouse colitis? (literature)</h3>
<p class="small">MGI curation is incomplete, so the literature was searched too (Europe PMC, title and abstract: the gene perturbed in a mouse colitis model). It says whether a gene was tested, not with which result. {{ third.mouse_lit_fic_untested }} of the {{ third.mouse_lit_fic }} first-in-class genes have no mouse colitis study: untested, and the natural next experiment.</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Gene</th><th class="num">Papers</th><th>Most cited (PMID)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in third.mouse_lit_rows %}<tr><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td class="num">{{ r.papers }}</td><td>{{ r.pmids or "—" }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>{% endif %}
<p class="small">Most alleles remove the gene, so "loss gives or worsens colitis" means the gene protects the mucosa (inhibiting it is a risk) and "loss protects" means it drives colitis (inhibition is the therapeutic direction). Numbers are mouse genotypes in MGI.</p>{% endif %}

{% if negctl %}<h2>Are the rankings just rewarding expressed or famous genes?</h2>
<p class="small">Each UC clinical target is compared only with non-target genes in the same tenth of mean expression (or of the Open Targets literature score), so a score that merely favours highly expressed or much-studied genes would fall to about 0.5. The last column is the 95th percentile of the expression-matched AUROC for 200 random gene sets of the same size.</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Score</th><th class="num">rho with mean expression</th><th class="num">rho with literature score</th><th class="num">AUROC, expression-matched</th><th class="num">AUROC, literature-matched</th><th class="num">Random sets, 95th percentile</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for k in ["convergence", "target profile", "biomarker profile"] %}{% set v = negctl[k] %}<tr><td>{{ k }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.rho_mean_expression }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.rho_literature_score }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.auroc_expression_matched }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.auroc_literature_matched }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.null_expression_matched_p95 }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">Expression level does not explain the recovery of clinical targets. Matching on the literature score lowers it ({{ "%.2f" | format([negctl["convergence"], negctl["target profile"], negctl["biomarker profile"]] | map(attribute="auroc_literature_matched") | min) }}–{{ "%.2f" | format([negctl["convergence"], negctl["target profile"], negctl["biomarker profile"]] | map(attribute="auroc_literature_matched") | max) }}), so part of the signal is that clinical targets are well-studied genes; the scores still beat random sets clearly.</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Two questions, two scores</h2>
<p class="small">The overall score mixes target and biomarker evidence, so there are two separate profiles. The <strong>target</strong> profile weighs ({% for k, v in profile_weights.target.items() %}{{ k | replace("_", " ") }} {{ v }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}). The <strong>biomarker</strong> profile weighs ({% for k, v in profile_weights.biomarker.items() %}{{ k | replace("_", " ") }} {{ v }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}). Neither uses literature or drug history, which lets us test them against clinical targets below without the answer leaking into the score.</p>
<div class="howbox calc"><h4>What the components of each profile mean</h4>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Profile</th><th>Component</th><th>How it is calculated (each runs from 0 to 1)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Target</td><td>expression up</td><td>Like the expression column, but only increases in UC count.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Target</td><td>tissue change</td><td>Share of the bulk change kept after the composition adjustment, when the adjusted change is significant. It rewards changes the tissue itself makes rather than arriving cells.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Target</td><td>genetics</td><td>Open Targets germline association with UC.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Target</td><td>tractability predicted</td><td>1 if the protein looks tractable by a small molecule or antibody. Existing clinical drugs are not used, so the score does not know the answer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biomarker</td><td>expression any</td><td>Like the expression column, counting increases and decreases.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biomarker</td><td>replication</td><td>As the replication column.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biomarker</td><td>activity</td><td>min(1, |ρ| ÷ 0.4) for the strongest significant correlation with the Mayo subscore.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biomarker</td><td>inflammation</td><td>As the inflammation column (inflamed vs non-inflamed).</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></div>
{% for name in ["target", "biomarker"] %}{% if profiles[name] %}
<h3>{{ name | capitalize }} profile, top 25 {{ q('profile_table') }}</h3>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Gene</th><th>Score</th>{% for k in profile_weights[name] %}<th>{{ k | replace("_", " ") }}</th>{% endfor %}<th>Rank 90 % interval</th><th>P(top 10)</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>{% for r in profiles[name][:25] %}{% set allids = [] %}{% for v in r.evidence_ids.values() %}{% for i in v %}{% set _ = allids.append(i) %}{% endfor %}{% endfor %}
<tr class="claim" data-evidence="{{ allids | evattr }}"><td>{{ r.rank }}</td><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td class="num">{{ r.score }}</td>
{% for k in profile_weights[name] %}<td class="num">{{ r.components[k] | fmt }}</td>{% endfor %}
<td class="num">{% if r.rank_interval_90 %}{{ r.rank_interval_90.rank_p05 }}–{{ r.rank_interval_90.rank_p95 }}{% else %}—{% endif %}</td>
<td class="num">{% if r.rank_interval_90 %}{{ r.rank_interval_90.p_top10 }}{% else %}—{% endif %}</td>
<td>{{ allids[:6] | cite | safe }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
{% endif %}{% endfor %}

<h2>Robustness to the weights</h2>
{{ paras(f.narrative.sections.robustness or []) }}
<p class="small">Each ranking is recomputed 1,000 times with weights drawn at random around the chosen ones (each multiplied by a factor between 0.5 and 2). Dots are median ranks and bars cover the 5th to 95th percentile. A short bar near the top means the gene stays there whatever the weights.</p>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How to read the stability charts</h4><ul>
<li>Each row is a gene. The weights are changed at random 1,000 times, and each time the genes are ranked again.</li>
<li>The dot is the gene's median rank and the bar is the range from the 5th to the 95th percentile. The horizontal axis is rank on a log scale, so left is better.</li>
<li>A short bar near the left is a robust call. A long bar means the gene's position depends on how the weights were chosen, so do not trust its exact rank.</li></ul></div>
<div class="grid two">
  {{ plot('rank_charts', figs.rank_target) }}
  {{ plot('rank_charts', figs.rank_biomarker) }}
</div>
{{ plot('rank_charts', figs.rank_conv) }}
{% if score_sens %}<h3>Stability of the other scores</h3>
<p class="small">The same 1,000 random re-weightings applied to the scores of the Discovery, Biologics &amp; immune and Structure tabs. "Top 10 stable" counts genes of the configured top 10 that stay in the top 10 in at least 80 % of re-weightings; rho is the median rank correlation between the configured and re-weighted scores.</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Score</th><th class="num">Genes</th><th class="num">Top 10 stable</th><th class="num">Median rho</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for k, v in score_sens.items() %}<tr><td>{{ {"biologics": "Biologic suitability", "immune": "Immune target", "novelty": "Discovery novelty (ordering only)", "pockets": "Pocket score"}.get(k, k) }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.n }}</td><td class="num">{{ v.top10_stable }} / 10</td><td class="num">{{ v.median_rho }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">The overall order of every score is stable (rho ≥ 0.9). The exact top 10 of the novelty ordering is not: it only orders the discovery table, while the filters decide which genes are first-in-class.</p>{% endif %}

<h2>Evaluation against outcomes the scores do not see {{ q('outcomes') }}</h2>
<p class="small">Gold sets: {{ evals.gold_sets.uc_clinical_targets | length }} UC clinical targets (phase ≥ 2 with at least two trials or approval, drugs with ≤ 5 targets) and the {{ evals.gold_sets.temporal_wide_targets | length }} targets first tried in UC on or after {{ evals.gold_sets.cutoff_wide }} ({{ evals.gold_sets.temporal_wide_targets | join(", ") }}), with older UC targets removed from the universe. The convergence score includes literature and drug history, so its clinical-target AUROC is partly circular.</p>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How to read this evaluation</h4><ul>
<li>We take genes the clinic did use as targets in UC and ask whether each score ranks them above the other genes. AUROC is the chance that a true target outranks a random gene: 0.5 is a coin flip and 1.0 is perfect.</li>
<li>Recall at 100 is the share of true targets among the top 100 genes. The 95 % interval comes from resampling the true targets.</li>
<li>The target and biomarker profiles use no literature or drug history, so they could not simply look up the answer.</li>
<li>Clinical targets are mostly druggable proteins, so a score that only flags druggability also does well. The tractability rows show how much of the result that explains.</li>
<li>The post-2016 set is retrospective, because the data used today were not frozen in 2016. Read it as a hint, not as a proof.</li></ul></div>
{{ plot('outcomes', figs.outcomes) }}
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Gold set</th><th>Score</th><th>n</th><th>AUROC (95 % CI)</th><th>Recall@100</th><th>Median rank</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for key, label in [("clinical_targets", "UC clinical targets"), ("temporal_wide", "First UC trial ≥ " ~ (evals.gold_sets.cutoff_wide or "")[:4])] %}
{% for sname, m in evals[key].items() %}<tr><td>{{ label }}</td><td>{{ sname }}</td><td class="num">{{ m.n_gold }}</td>
<td class="num">{{ m.auroc }} ({{ m.auroc_ci95[0] }}–{{ m.auroc_ci95[1] }})</td><td class="num">{{ (m.recall_at_100 * 100) | round(0) | int }} %</td>
<td class="num">{{ "{:,}".format(m.median_rank) if m.median_rank is not none else "—" }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">Known-answer questions answered by an agent with the tools: {{ extra.qa }}. MR as a check: among the {{ G.plasma.clinical_tested }} UC clinical targets with a plasma instrument, {{ G.plasma.clinical_significant | join(", ") or "none" }} significant.</p>

{% set cb = baselines.closed_book %}{% set pl = baselines.pipeline %}
<h2>What does the agentic layer add? {{ q('baseline_table') }}</h2>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Closed-book LLM (no tools)</th><th>GraphRAG pipeline</th></tr></thead><tbody>
